										"paragraphStyle" : {
											"__type" : "textStyle"
										},
										"string" : "Main points of the LaTeX file:\n\n- It is a paper titled **“Towards Actionable Visualization: Ten Years Later, What Generative AI Changes and What It Cannot.”**\n- It revisits the authors’ 2016/2018 work on software visualization and argues that **generative AI changes the economics of visualization**: producing a visualization is becoming cheap, but **human interpretation, judgment, and trust remain expensive and essential**.\n- Core thesis: AI reduces the cost of generating custom, on-demand visualizations, so the research bottleneck shifts from inventing more visualization techniques to **helping humans perceive, inspect, and verify complex software and AI behavior**.\n\nKey arguments:\n\n- **Neglected domains now become tractable**: areas like rationale, contracts, and policies were previously underserved because preparing the data was too costly; AI can now help extract and structure that data.\n- **Tool sustainability may get worse before it gets better**: if AI makes prototype creation nearly free, academia may produce even more short-lived, undocumented, unreproducible tools unless incentives change.\n- **Immersive media increased**: compared with the earlier survey, papers using VR/AR-like delivery media appear to have grown substantially, though still remain a minority.\n\nProposed research agenda:\n\n- Shift **from technique catalogs to perceptual capacity**: study what makes generated views trustworthy and quickly interpretable for software tasks.\n- Shift **from monolithic tools to disposable, continuous seeing**: generate visualizations on demand for the question at hand, then discard them.\n- Treat **AI-generated code and AI reasoning traces** as a new object of software-visualization research.\n\nRisks emphasized:\n\n- AI-generated visualizations can be misleading, so they must remain **traceable to source data** and support drill-down inspection.\n- The field should not compete with machines on raw generation, but instead focus on **human strengths: perception, judgment, accountability, and trust**.\n\nConclusion:\n\n- The paper argues that actionable visualization is no longer mainly blocked by production cost.\n- Because AI makes visualization generation cheap, the field should focus on making visualizations **trustworthy, inspectable, and useful for human decision-making**.\n- Its closing claim is that the field may be close to dropping “Towards” and aiming directly for **visualization that is already actionable**."
									}
								]
